(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-08-prognosis-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 8: Prognosis Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"For a study of the risk of esophageal cancer in patients with Barrett esophagus (a precancerous lesion), which of the following times in the course of disease is the best example of zero time?","choices":["Diagnosis of Barrett esophagus for each patient","Death of each patient","Diagnosis of esophageal cancer for each patient","Calendar time when the first patient is enrolled in the study","Calendar time when no patient remains in the study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Zero time is the point in the disease course at which every member of an inception cohort is assembled, ideally as early and as uniformly as possible. Here that is the diagnosis of Barrett esophagus, the condition that defines the cohort, and esophageal cancer is the outcome being followed. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.1"},{"q":"A cohort study describes the recurrence of seizure within 1 year in children hospitalized with a first febrile seizure. It compared recurrence in children who had infection versus immunization as an underlying cause for fever at the time of the first seizure. Some of the children were no longer in the study when outcome (a second seizure) was assessed at 1 year. Which of the following would have the greatest effect on study results?","choices":["Why the children dropped out","When in the course of follow-up the children left the study","Whether dropping out was related to prognosis","Whether the number of children dropping out is similar in the groups"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Migration bias arises when patients drop out and those who leave are not similar to those who stay. Dropping out distorts the results only if it is related to prognosis, for example if children who were more likely to have a second seizure left; as the proportion of dropouts grows, so does the potential for bias. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 23; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.2"},{"q":"A cohort study of prostate cancer care compares rates of incontinence in patients who were treated with surgery versus medical care alone. Incontinence is assessed from review of medical records. Which of the following is not an example of measurement bias?","choices":["Men were more likely to tell their surgeon about incontinence.","Surgeons were less likely to record complications of their surgery in the record.","Men who got surgery were more likely to have follow-up visits.","Chart abstractors used their judgment is deciding whether incontinence was present or not.","Rates of incontinence were higher in the men who got surgery."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A higher rate of incontinence in men who had surgery is the study result, and it can reflect a true effect of surgery. Measurement bias means one group is more likely to have its outcome detected or classified differently, so a difference in rates is not itself a measurement error. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.3"},{"q":"A clinical prediction rule has been developed to classify the prognosis of community-acquired pneumonia. Which of the following is most characteristic of such a clinical prediction rule?","choices":["Calculating a score is simple.","The clinical data are readily available.","Multiple prognostic factors are included.","The results are used to guide further management of the patient.","All of the above."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A clinical prediction rule estimates the probability of an outcome from a set of patient characteristics (multiple prognostic factors, which predict better than any single data point), and such rules are also simple to score, use readily available clinical data and guide further management. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.4"},{"q":"A study describes the clinical course of patients who have an uncommon neurologic disease. Patients are identified at a referral center that specializes in this disease. Their medical records are reviewed for patients' characteristics, treatments, and their current disease status. Which of the following best describes this kind of study?","choices":["Cohort study","Case-control study","Case series","Cross-sectional study","A randomized controlled trial"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A case series is a descriptive design that reports the characteristics of a group of patients, here those seen at a referral center with an uncommon disease, without an inception cohort, a comparison group or a representative sample. It gives a description of the clinical course, not relative risk. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8; 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.5"},{"q":"A study used time-to-event analysis to describe the survival from diagnosis of 100 patients with congestive heart failure. By the third year, 60 patients have been censored. Which of the following would not be a reason for one of these patients being censored?","choices":["The patient died of another cause before year 3.","The patient decided not to continue in the study.","The patient had been enrolled in the study for less than 3 years.","The patient developed another disease that could be fatal."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Developing another potentially fatal disease does not remove anyone from observation. Patients are censored when they leave the study or have not been followed for the full period; a patient who has another disease but is still alive and being followed remains in the analysis. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.6"},{"q":"Which of the following kinds of studies cannot be used to identify prognostic factors?","choices":["Prevalence study","Time-to-event analysis","Case-control study","Cohort study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A prevalence study assesses a population for a condition at one point in time, so it measures no events over time. Prognosis is about outcomes of disease over time, so this design cannot identify prognostic factors (prevalence studies are also poor for cause and effect and better for developing hypotheses). — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.7"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes the information in a survival curve?","choices":["An unbiased estimate of survival even if some patients leave the study","The estimated probability of survival from zero time","The proportion of a cohort still alive at the end of follow-up","The rate at which original members of the cohort leave the study","The cumulative survival of a cohort over time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A survival curve gives the estimated probability of surviving to any point in time, calculated from the cumulative probability of surviving each preceding interval, starting at zero time. It estimates survival for a hypothetical rather than an actual cohort. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 18; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.8"},{"q":"Which of the following is the most appropriate sample for a study of prognosis?","choices":["Members of the general population","Patients in primary care in the community","Patients admitted to a community hospital","Patients referred to a specialist","It depends on who will use the results of the study."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The right sample depends on the question: referral pattern (academic medical center versus community) affects who is in the study, and results apply only to patients similar to those the reader sees. Prognosis in primary care and among those referred to specialists are both useful to know. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.9"},{"q":"Investigators wish to describe the clinical course of multiple sclerosis. They take advantage of a clinical trial, already completed, in which control patients received usual care. Patients in the trial had been identified at referral centers, had been enrolled at the time of diagnosis, and had met rigorous entry criteria. After 10 years, all patients had been examined yearly and remained under observation, and 40% were still able to walk. Which of the following most limits the credibility of this study?","choices":["Inconsistent zero time","Generalizability","Measurement bias","Migration bias","Failure to use time-to-event methods"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients had to meet stringent trial entry criteria at referral centers, so the usual-care patients are not representative of people with multiple sclerosis in the general population or in a defined clinical setting. Generalizability is often limited because study patients may not be representative of other populations. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.10"},{"q":"A cohort study is done to assess how often headaches are present in children who have had concussions. At the scheduled 5-year follow-up, one- fifth of the participants have dropped out of the study. The investigators consider calculating the incidence of headaches using multiple imputation. Which of the following is correct?","choices":["Multiple imputation assumes that the characteristics measured in the study can be used to reliably estimate the missing values.","Multiple imputation removes the effects of bias from incomplete follow-up.","Multiple imputation can estimate headache rates for the missing patients by randomly selecting whether headache is present in patients with complete follow- up.","Complete case analysis compares the results for patients with complete information to results for those with missing values."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Multiple imputation is a technique for estimating missing values by statistical modeling of the patients with complete data, taking their other characteristics into account. It therefore assumes that the measured characteristics can reliably predict what is missing. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.11"},{"q":"Many different clinical prediction rules have been developed to assess the severity of community-acquired pneumonia. Which of the following is the most important reason for choosing one to use?","choices":["The prediction rule classifies patients into groups with very different prognosis.","The prediction rule has been validated in different settings.","Many variables are included in the rule.","Prognostic factors include state-of-the-science diagnostic tests.","The score is calculated using computers."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Even a rule built with the best methods is most strongly tested by showing that it predicts outcomes in patients other than those used to develop it. Study patients must be similar to your own for a rule to apply, so validation in different settings is the most important reason. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.12"},{"q":"In a study of patients who smoke and developed peripheral arterial disease, the hazard ratio for amputation in patients who continued to smoke, relative to those who quit smoking, is 5. Which of the following best characterizes the hazard ratio?","choices":["In this study, it is the rate of continued smoking divided by the rate of quitting.","It can be estimated from a case-control study of smoking and amputation.","It cannot be adjusted for the presence or absence of other factors related to prognosis.","It conveys information similar to relative risk.","It is calculated from the cumulative incidence of amputation in smokers and quitters."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The hazard ratio from a Cox survival analysis can be interpreted like a relative risk: a ratio of 5 means patients who continued to smoke have five times the risk of amputation compared with those who quit; a ratio below 1 would mean protection. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.13"},{"q":"In a time-to-event analyses, the event:","choices":["Can occur only once","Is dichotomous","Both A and B","Neither A nor B"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The outcome event in a time-to-event analysis is dichotomous (it occurs or it does not, such as living versus dying) and it can occur only once, so both statements are true. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 49; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.14"}]};
